# 🖱️ AutoClicker for macOS

A powerful and elegant autoclicker application for macOS with support for both mouse clicks and keyboard key presses. Built with Python and native macOS frameworks.

![macOS](https://img.shields.io/badge/macOS-10.13+-blue.svg)
![Python](https://img.shields.io/badge/Python-3.7+-green.svg)
![License](https://img.shields.io/badge/license-MIT-orange.svg)

## ✨ Features

### Mouse Mode
- **Click Speed Control**: Configure clicks per second or per minute
- **Button Selection**: Choose between left, middle, or right mouse button
- **Smart Click**: Option to pause clicking when mouse is moving
- **Customizable Delays**: Set start delay and auto-stop timer
- **Click at Current Position**: Clicks wherever your mouse cursor is located

### Keyboard Mode
- **Key Press Automation**: Automatically press any keyboard key
- **Custom Key Selection**: Choose any key from your keyboard
- **Same Speed Controls**: Use the same timing configurations as mouse mode

### General Features
- **Custom Hotkey**: Set your own hotkey combination to start/stop (default: F8)
- **Menu Bar Integration**: Lives in your macOS menu bar for easy access
- **Modern UI**: Beautiful gradient interface with dark theme
- **Countdown Timer**: Visual feedback when starting with delay
- **Status Indicators**: Real-time status updates with color coding

## 📋 Requirements

- macOS 10.13 (High Sierra) or later
- Python 3.7 or higher
- PyObjC framework
- pynput library

## 🚀 Installation

1. **Clone the repository**
   ```bash
   git clone https://github.com/BGexe/autoclicker.git
   cd autoclicker-macos
   ```

2. **Install dependencies**
   ```bash
   pip3 install pyobjc pyobjc-framework-Cocoa pyobjc-framework-Quartz pynput
   ```

3. **Grant accessibility permissions**
   - Go to System Preferences → Security & Privacy → Privacy → Accessibility
   - Add Terminal or your Python interpreter to the allowed apps
   - This is required for the app to simulate clicks and key presses

## 💻 Usage

1. **Run the application**
   ```bash
   python3 autoclicker.py
   ```

2. **Configure your settings**
   - Click the 🖱️ AutoClicker icon in the menu bar
   - Set your desired click/key press speed
   - Choose between Mouse Mode or Keyboard Mode
   - Configure optional delays and smart options

3. **Start clicking/pressing**
   - Click the "Start" button or press your hotkey (default: F8)
   - Press the hotkey again to stop

## ⚙️ Configuration Options

### Speed Settings
- **Clicks/Presses per unit**: Number of actions (1-1000+)
- **Time unit**: Choose between "second" or "minute"

### Delays
- **Start after**: Wait X seconds before starting (0 = immediate)
- **Stop after**: Automatically stop after X seconds (0 = infinite)

### Mouse Mode Options
- **Button type**: Left, Middle, or Right mouse button
- **Smart click**: Only click when mouse hasn't moved for X seconds

### Keyboard Mode Options
- **Key selection**: Click "Select key" and press any key on your keyboard

### Hotkey
- Click the hotkey button and press your desired key combination
- Supports modifier keys (Cmd, Ctrl, Alt, Shift) + any key

## 🎮 Controls

- **F8** (default): Start/Stop the autoclicker
- **Custom hotkey**: Set your own combination in the interface
- **Start/Stop button**: Manual control from the interface
- **❌ button**: Quit the application

## 🔒 Permissions

This app requires the following macOS permissions:

1. **Accessibility**: To simulate mouse clicks and keyboard presses
2. **Input Monitoring**: To detect hotkey presses

You'll be prompted to grant these permissions on first run.

## 🛠️ Troubleshooting

### App doesn't click/press keys
- Make sure you've granted Accessibility permissions
- Restart the app after granting permissions

### Hotkey doesn't work
- Check that Input Monitoring permission is granted
- Try a different hotkey combination
- Ensure no other app is using the same hotkey

### App doesn't appear in menu bar
- Check if the app is running in Terminal
- Look for error messages in the console
- Try restarting the app

## 📝 Technical Details

### Built With
- **PyObjC**: Python-Objective-C bridge for native macOS integration
- **Cocoa Framework**: For UI components and menu bar integration
- **Quartz**: For low-level mouse event simulation
- **pynput**: For keyboard event listening and simulation

### Architecture
- Single-threaded event loop using NSRunLoop
- Native NSPopover for the interface
- CGEvent API for mouse simulation
- Keyboard listener running in daemon thread

